---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { notebooks, forthcomingReports } from '../content/projects';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<Layout title="Data Analytics" description="Explore Monalisa Hota’s Kaggle notebooks, ASER 2005 contribution, Indian Social Institute pilot study, LSE dissertation, CEG outreach experiment, and Arlington town survey.">
  <section class="page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Data analytics and social research</p><h1>Good questions. Grounded answers.</h1><p class="lead">Notebooks, education research, and social policy, plus the CEG outreach experiment and the Arlington town survey.</p></section>
  <nav class="section-nav" aria-label="Analytics sections"><a href="#notebooks">Kaggle notebooks</a><a href="#aser">Pratham / ASER</a><a href="#isi">Indian Social Institute</a><a href="#thesis">LSE thesis</a><a href={`${base}analytics/ceg/`}>CEG experiment</a><a href="#town-survey">Town survey</a><a href="#reports">More reports</a></nav>
  <section id="notebooks"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">LEARNING BY DOING</p><h2>The notebook <em>collection.</em></h2></div><a class="text-link" href="https://www.kaggle.com/lisamlh/code">All notebooks on Kaggle ↗</a></div><div class="grid three">{notebooks.map((notebook, i) => <article class="notebook-card"><div class="notebook-art" aria-hidden="true"><span>In [0{i + 1}]:</span><code>{['explore → understand', 'analyse → communicate', 'observe → reflect'][i]}</code><div class="mini-bars">{[35, 65, 45, 90, 55, 75, 100].map((height, j) => <i style={`height:${height}%;opacity:${.4 + j * .08}`} />)}</div></div><div class="card-body"><p class="eyebrow">KAGGLE / ANALYTICAL PRACTICE</p><h3>{notebook.title}</h3><p>{notebook.description}</p><a class="text-link" href={`https://www.kaggle.com/code/lisamlh/${notebook.slug}`}>Read the notebook ↗</a></div></article>)}</div></section>
  <section id="aser" class="research split"><div class="report-cover rose"><span>PRATHAM / EDUCATION</span><strong>ASER</strong><span class="cover-year">2005</span><span>ANNUAL STATUS OF<br />EDUCATION REPORT</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">PRATHAM / NATIONAL EDUCATION RESEARCH</p><h2>Making learning<br /><em>visible.</em></h2><p class="lead">Contributed data analytics work to the Annual Status of Education Report (ASER) 2005, in 2005.</p><p>A contribution to a collaborative national report on children’s education in India. Her later Research Officer role at Pratham (2006–2007) included survey tools, surveyor training, data collection, cleaning, and quantitative analysis using Stata.</p><div class="tags"><span>Education research</span><span>Data analytics</span><span>Collaborative report</span></div><a class="button" href="https://img.asercentre.org/docs/Publications/ASER%20Reports/ASER_2005/aserfullreport2005.pdf">Read ASER 2005 · PDF ↗</a></div></section>
  <section id="isi" class="research split"><div><p class="eyebrow">INDIAN SOCIAL INSTITUTE / SEPTEMBER 2012 REPORT</p><h2>How do values<br /><em>take shape?</em></h2><p class="lead">Evolution of Values: A Pilot Study in a School in Bangalore.</p><p>Designed thematic moral-dilemma stories and conducted qualitative research with 37 children aged 6–15. The study explored integrity, empathy, greed, and perceptions of gender and caste across three age groups.</p><p>Analysis identified patterns in reasoning, expression, and empathy, and raised questions for future research. This was an exploratory, single-school pilot, rather than a statistically representative study.</p><div class="tags"><span>Qualitative research</span><span>Study design</span><span>Child development</span></div><a class="button" href={`${base}documents/evolution-of-values.pdf`}>Read the pilot report · PDF ↗</a></div><div class="study-art"><p class="eyebrow">A STUDY BUILT AROUND STORIES</p><div class="study-stat">37<small>children</small></div><div class="age-groups"><span>6–8<small>years</small></span><span>9–12<small>years</small></span><span>13–15<small>years</small></span></div><p>Integrity · Empathy · Greed<br />Reasoning · Expression · Fairness</p></div></section>
  <section id="thesis" class="research split"><div class="report-cover teal"><span>LONDON SCHOOL OF ECONOMICS</span><strong>Breaking<br />the silence.</strong><span class="cover-year">2009</span><span>SOCIAL POLICY & DEVELOPMENT<br />MASTER’S DISSERTATION</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">MSc / SOCIAL POLICY AND DEVELOPMENT</p><h2>Reporting Child Sexual Abuse in India</h2><p class="lead">Lessons from Developed Nations</p><p>A comparative analysis of reporting and child-protection approaches in India, the United States, and the United Kingdom, drawing on literature and secondary sources.</p><p>The dissertation examines barriers to disclosure, the role of professionals, and opportunities for coordinated, child-sensitive reporting systems. Its recommendations emphasise connected services, professional training, and approaches adapted to local contexts.</p><p class="small">Submitted September 2009. The analysis reflects the policy and legal context at that time.</p><div class="tags"><span>Literature review</span><span>Comparative policy analysis</span></div><a class="button" href={`${base}documents/lse-dissertation.pdf`}>Read the dissertation · PDF ↗</a></div></section>
  <section id="town-survey" class="research split"><div class="report-cover teal"><span>ENVISION ARLINGTON</span><strong>2025<br />Town Survey</strong><span class="cover-year">2024–2026</span><span>CIVIC ENGAGEMENT GROUP</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Community research</p><h2>Arlington’s annual town survey</h2><p class="lead">Contributed to the annual town survey through survey tool development, data analysis, visualisation, and report writing with the Civic Engagement Group.</p><p>The 2025 report summarises 3,268 responses collected online and on paper from 1 January to 4 March 2024. Topics include trees, civic engagement, fossil fuels, Arlington Community Media, education, and diversity, equity, and inclusion.</p><p class="small">The published report names AI tools and software used in its preparation, including Gemini, Jasper, Tableau, Excel, and Perplexity. Figures here are taken from that report. Her name is not printed in it.</p><div class="tags"><span>Survey tools</span><span>Analysis</span><span>Visualisation</span><span>Report writing</span></div><a class="button" href={`${base}documents/2025-town-survey.pdf`}>Read the 2025 report · PDF ↗</a></div></section>
  <section id="reports"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">More from the research journey</p><h2>Work with purpose.</h2></div><p>Further contributions from education and child rights. Supporting reports will be added here.</p></div><div class="grid three">{forthcomingReports.map(report => <article class="work-card"><p class="eyebrow">{report.label}</p><h3>{report.title}</h3><p>{report.description}</p><span class="status">Report to be added</span></article>)}</div></section>
</Layout>
